course: euphemisms
lesson: 15
title: Soft words for hard things: children's behaviour and school reports and teachers
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Parents and teachers often describe a child's problems in kind, positive-sounding words so the child and family are not hurt.
explain: On a school report «sociable» may mean «talks too much in class», and «lively» may mean «cannot sit still and disturbs others».
explain: Some words are fond («rascal», «full of beans»), some are teacher jargon («working towards», «emerging»), and some are ironic («little darling» said through gritted teeth).
explain: Vivid example: a report says «Max has a strong personality and a lot of energy», which a parent can read as «Max is bossy and never sits still».
fact: "Having a moment" said about a child means throwing a tantrum.
fact: "Boisterous" means rough and noisy.
fact: "Full of beans" means very energetic, almost hyperactive (UK).
fact: "Free spirit" said about a child means one who does not follow rules.
fact: "Clingy" means anxious and unwilling to leave the parent.
fact: "Slow to warm up" means shy or anxious with new people.
fact: "Testing boundaries" means misbehaving on purpose to see what is allowed.
fact: "Acting out" means behaving badly because of upset feelings.
fact: "Live wire" means a very energetic, hyperactive child (one so full of energy that he cannot keep still).
fact: "Cheeky" means rude or disrespectful, often said lightly (UK).
fact: "Little darling" said ironically means a badly behaved child.
fact: "Rascal" means a mischievous, naughty child, said fondly.
fact: "Hangry" means cross because of hunger (jokey).
fact: "Quiet time" means a rest or calming down that the child must take.
fact: "Lively" on a school report often means disruptive or hyperactive.
fact: "Sociable" on a school report often means talks too much in class.
fact: "Has a lot of energy" on a report often means cannot sit still.
fact: "Strong personality" on a report often means bossy or defiant.
fact: "Working towards" on a UK report means not yet meeting the expected standard.
fact: "Emerging" on a UK report means well below the expected level.
exercise: At the party Dad says, "Ella's having a moment." What does he mean? | Ella is throwing a tantrum | Ella is having fun | Ella is taking a photo | Ella is resting for a second
exercise: The coach calls the boys "boisterous." What does he mean? | They are rough and noisy | They are very polite and quiet | They are good at boxing | They are clever
exercise: Gran says Ollie is "full of beans" today. What does she mean? | He is very energetic | He ate too many beans | He has a tummy ache | He is very sleepy
exercise: Mum calls her daughter "a free spirit." What does she usually mean? | She does not follow rules | She costs nothing | She believes in ghosts and spirits | She likes being alone
exercise: The nursery says Leo is "clingy" in the mornings. What does that mean? | He is anxious and will not let go of his parent | He climbs on all the furniture in the nursery room | He has sticky hands | He is cold
exercise: "She's slow to warm up," says Dad when his girl hides behind him. What does he mean? | She is shy with new people | She is always cold | She is slow at running | She takes a while to wake up
exercise: The teacher says, "He's testing boundaries." What does she mean? | He misbehaves on purpose to see what is allowed | He is measuring the playground with a long tape | He is taking a geography test | He is checking the fences
exercise: "Since the divorce he's been acting out." What does that mean? | He is behaving badly because he is upset | He is playing the main part in a school play | He is playing outside | He is acting in films
exercise: The teacher calls Zara "a live wire." What does she mean? | Zara is very energetic and never still | Zara is very good at fixing electric wires | Zara is dangerous to touch | Zara is very thin
exercise: "Don't be cheeky," says the teacher. What did the child do? | Said something rude or disrespectful | Puffed out his cheeks like a balloon | Smiled too much | Ate too fast
exercise: The tired teacher says, "Thank you, little darling," after a boy throws paint. What does she really mean? | He is behaving badly | He is very sweet | He is her favourite | He is tiny
exercise: Grandpa calls his grandson "a little rascal." What does he mean? | A naughty but loved child | A small, scruffy dog | A shy child | A sick child
exercise: "Sorry, I'm just hangry," says Sam after shouting. What does he mean? | He is cross because he is hungry | He is hanging upside down from a tree | He is angry about a hanger | He is tired from running
exercise: Mum says, "It's quiet time now." What must the child do? | Rest and calm down | Play a quiet game outside | Whisper a secret | Listen to loud music
exercise: A report says, "Jack is a lively member of the class." What may it really mean? | Jack is disruptive and cannot settle | Jack is the healthiest pupil in the whole year | Jack sings in the choir | Jack lives near school
exercise: A report says, "Amy is very sociable." What may it really mean? | Amy talks too much in class | Amy is the class president | Amy likes social studies | Amy goes to many parties
exercise: A report says, "Noah has a lot of energy." What may it really mean? | Noah cannot sit still | Noah eats well | Noah is the fastest runner | Noah never gets ill
exercise: A report says, "Lucy has a strong personality." What may it really mean? | Lucy is bossy or defiant | Lucy is very strong for her age | Lucy is famous | Lucy is very funny
exercise: A UK report says a child is "working towards" the standard in maths. What does that mean? | The child is not yet meeting the expected standard | The child is above the standard and needs harder work | The child is doing extra homework | The child walks to maths class
exercise: A UK report says a child is "emerging" in reading. What does that mean? | The child is well below the expected level | The child is the best reader | The child is just coming into the class late each day | The child reads under water
test: Parents' evening: "Sophie is very sociable, she always has something to say to her neighbour." What is the teacher saying? | Sophie chats too much in class | Sophie is popular and well behaved | Sophie has many friends at home | Sophie is good at speaking French
test: School report: "Writing: emerging." How is the child doing in writing? | Well below the expected level | At the expected level for her age | Above the expected level | Getting top marks
test: Mum to Dad, rough: "Your son's 'testing boundaries' again. He drew on the wall, the little sod." What happened? | The boy misbehaved on purpose | The boy measured the room | The boy did his homework | The boy painted a picture he was told to
test: Kid: "omg i'm sooo hangry, gimme ur crisps" What is going on? | The kid is cross because of hunger | The kid is hanging off a climbing bar | The kid is happy | The kid is sick
test: Teacher in the staff room: "Oh, Mason's a live wire alright. Never sat still once." What is Mason like? | Very hyperactive | Very calm | Very ill | Good at science
test: Nursery worker: "She's just having a moment, give her a minute." What is the child doing? | Having a tantrum | Taking a nap | Playing a game | Counting to sixty
test: Report: "Ethan has a strong personality and likes to lead games his way." What may it really mean? | Ethan is bossy | Ethan is the best athlete | Ethan is quiet and shy | Ethan is very strong
test: Dad, sarcastic: "Oh, here comes the little darling," as his son slams the door. What does Dad mean? | The son is behaving badly | The son is sweet | The son is small | The son is a baby
test: School psychologist: "Since his dad left, Toby has been acting out in class." What does it mean? | Toby misbehaves because he is upset | Toby has a part in the school play | Toby acts very well | Toby is often absent
test: Gran: "Full of beans this morning, aren't we? Up at five!" What is Gran saying? | The child is full of energy | The child ate beans for breakfast | The child is sick | The child is sleepy
test: Uncle: "Boisterous? Mate, they wrecked the garden." What does it tell you? | The children were rough and noisy | The children were quiet and polite | The children did the gardening | The children were asleep
test: Teacher: "Report says 'working towards' in maths. Not a fail, but not there yet." What is true? | The child is below the expected standard | The child is well above the expected standard | The child has finished maths | The child changed classes
test: Mum at the school gate: "He's slow to warm up, he'll talk to you after a while." What is the boy like? | Shy with new people | Always cold | Slow at sports | Late every morning
exam: Identify the meaning most accurately conveyed by the remark in a school report that a pupil "has a lot of energy". | The pupil experiences difficulty in remaining seated | The pupil is in excellent physical health | The pupil excels in athletic competition | The pupil consumes a nutritious diet
exam: It is given that a pupil's attainment in reading is recorded as "working towards" the expected standard. Ascertain the attainment thereby indicated. | Attainment that does not yet meet the expected standard | Attainment that exceeds the expected standard | Attainment that fully meets the expected standard | Attainment that cannot be assessed
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of the term "clingy" in respect of a young child. | The child is anxious and reluctant to be parted from the parent | The child habitually climbs upon furniture | The child's hands are adhesive owing to the consumption of confectionery | The child is susceptible to the cold
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a parent's description of a child as "a free spirit"? | The child does not readily conform to rules | The child is provided without charge | The child professes belief in apparitions | The child prefers solitude to company
